      "pronunciationText": "Every atom has a tiny, dense centre called the nucleus. It holds protons, which each carry a charge of plus one, and neutrons, which carry no charge at all. Protons and neutrons have almost the same mass, so we give each a relative mass of one. Around the nucleus are the electrons. Each has a charge of minus one, but its mass is less than a thousandth of a proton's, so we count it as almost zero. This drawing is a model, not to scale. A real nucleus is far smaller, compared with the whole atom.",
