      "pronunciationText": "What about electrons? In a neutral atom, the plus and minus charges cancel, so the number of electrons equals the number of protons. This chlorine atom has seventeen electrons. But in salt, chlorine isn't a neutral atom. It has gained one extra electron, so it carries a charge of minus one. That makes it an ion, a charged particle, written with a small minus sign at the top right. The chloride ion has seventeen protons and eighteen electrons. Notice what didn't change. The protons, and so the element, stayed exactly the same.",
