      "requestAttempts": 1,
      "pronunciationText": "Isotopes can also differ in another way. Carbon-twelve and carbon-thirteen are stable, but carbon-fourteen is unstable, or radioactive. Sooner or later, its nucleus breaks down and gives out radiation. Why? Inside a nucleus, the positive protons repel each other. A very short-range attraction, called the strong nuclear force, holds all the protons and neutrons together. Neutrons add that attraction without adding any repulsion. Small nuclei are stable with roughly equal numbers of protons and neutrons, while heavy ones need extra neutrons, like lead-two-hundred-and-eight, with eighty-two protons and a hundred and twenty-six neutrons. With too many or too few neutrons, a nucleus is unstable. What it gives out when it breaks down is for a later video.",
